@IgorMinar, this is a valid point, but the inverse also applies - if an application had control over `NgZone.run()` calls that exhibit the problem described in #39348, it could already work around it by wrapping the call in a single `NgZone.run()`:

```typescript
// However, this issue only happens when NgZone is being entered for the first time.
// NgZone is aware of nesting, so if it's being re-entered, it will still wait
// for the outermost call to finish before doing change detection.
this.ngZone.run(() => {
  this.ngZone.runOutsideAngular(() => {

    // This code results in only one change detection occuring.
    for (let i = 0; i < 10; i++) {
      this.ngZone.run(() => {
        console.log("run");
      });
    }
  });
});
```

I've across variations of this issues where we have no control over these calls.
So, IMHO, providing something like `NgZone.runAsync()` wouldn't really add much value, though it is still better than the above workaround as the outermost `NgZone.run()` call would ALWAYS result in a change detection even if there are no `NgZone.run()` calls downstream.

While there's code that may expect change detection to happen immediately before `NgZone.run()` returns, this is both rare and incorrect assumption. If the call is nested inside another `NgZone.run()` call, it no longer holds true.

On the other hand, having change detection happen in another frame / VM turn entirely may be problematic.
Perhaps there's a middle ground between the immediacy of doing change detection synchronously and scheduling it to happen in another frame. What about scheduling it to run as microtask at the end of the current VM turn?

issue_context.body:
### Summary

Using `NgZone.run()` can in some cases result in a large number of change detections performed synchronously, creating a serious performance issue. 


### Repro 

https://stackblitz.com/edit/angular-5zht6w

```javascript
this.ngZone.runOutsideAngular(() => {
  setTimeout(() => {

    // This code results in one change detection occurring per
    // ngZone.run() call. This is entirely feasible, and can be a serious
    // performance issue.
    for (let i = 0; i < 100; i++) {
      this.ngZone.run(() => {
        // do something
      });
    }

  });
});
```

### Description

`NgZone` runs change detection as soon as the execution leaves the top-most `NgZone` call and all the microtasks there have finished, but if there are more than one top-level calls to `NgZone.run()` in a single VM turn, a synchronous change detection is run after each one. 

The example above may seem somewhat contrived, but this situation is quite easy to get into w/o even noticing. For example, you may have a  `window.onmessage` event handler that receives data from a worker or an `IFRAME`. The handler then iterates through the array of received objects, invoking various callbacks or emitting observable values for each one. If one of those callbacks or subscribers is part of an Angular app and needs to update something that should be picked up by Angular's change detection, they would wrap it in the `NgZone.run()` call.

